{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"As of November 6, 2026, Rule 6c-11(b) provides four categories of relief, subject to the rule’s conditions:\n\n1. **Creation-unit redemptions:** ETFs may redeem shares only in creation units rather than individually.\n2. **Market-price trading:** ETF shares may trade on the secondary market at negotiated market prices rather than NAV, notwithstanding Section 22(d) and Rule 22c-1.\n3. **Limited affiliated transactions:** Exemptions from Sections 17(a)(1)–(2) permit in-kind basket deposits and receipts involving persons affiliated solely through voting ownership of at least 5% of the ETF or an affiliated investment company, including specified second-tier affiliates. This is **not blanket relief** for other affiliate relationships or transactions.\n4. **Delayed foreign-investment delivery:** Section 22(e) relief permits delivery beyond seven days when foreign-market holidays or extended local delivery cycles prevent timely delivery—but **never beyond 15 days after tender**. It does not excuse delays for unrelated operational reasons."}}
